WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.220 --> 00:00:01.590
MAX SALTONSTALL: Hey, Steve,
my new app, Moneymaker,

00:00:01.590 --> 00:00:02.265
is doing great.

00:00:02.265 --> 00:00:04.590
It turns out, a lot of
people want to trade stocks

00:00:04.590 --> 00:00:05.340
from their phones.

00:00:05.340 --> 00:00:08.007
But now I've collected all these
account numbers and credit card

00:00:08.007 --> 00:00:08.640
numbers.

00:00:08.640 --> 00:00:10.332
I'm pretty worried
about protecting

00:00:10.332 --> 00:00:11.790
all that sensitive
information so I

00:00:11.790 --> 00:00:13.440
can maintain the trust
of everyone using my app.

00:00:13.440 --> 00:00:14.190
STEVE SOLEIMANI:
It sounds like you

00:00:14.190 --> 00:00:15.780
need some additional security.

00:00:15.780 --> 00:00:17.322
What if one of your
employees decides

00:00:17.322 --> 00:00:19.860
to go rogue and start
their own competition?

00:00:19.860 --> 00:00:20.880
MAX SALTONSTALL: Oh, no!

00:00:20.880 --> 00:00:23.520
I definitely need to minimize
any data exfiltration,

00:00:23.520 --> 00:00:25.982
either from a disgruntled
employee or any attacker.

00:00:25.982 --> 00:00:26.940
STEVE SOLEIMANI: Great!

00:00:26.940 --> 00:00:29.340
How do you control
data access today?

00:00:29.340 --> 00:00:31.990
MAX SALTONSTALL: Well, I've
got some sensitive data.

00:00:31.990 --> 00:00:34.548
But it's storage that many
projects have access to it.

00:00:34.548 --> 00:00:36.840
I'm really worried about
unauthorized copying going on.

00:00:36.840 --> 00:00:37.683
Can you help?

00:00:37.683 --> 00:00:38.850
STEVE SOLEIMANI: Absolutely.

00:00:38.850 --> 00:00:41.100
I'll show you how a VPC
Service Controls perimeter

00:00:41.100 --> 00:00:42.520
can make you more secure.

00:00:42.520 --> 00:00:43.478
MAX SALTONSTALL: Great.

00:00:49.345 --> 00:00:50.470
STEVE SOLEIMANI: All right.

00:00:50.470 --> 00:00:52.030
We've got two projects--

00:00:52.030 --> 00:00:54.490
one that has sensitive
data and one that

00:00:54.490 --> 00:00:57.250
is attempting to
exfiltrate data.

00:00:57.250 --> 00:01:00.280
Each project has its
own storage bucket.

00:01:00.280 --> 00:01:02.770
The sensitive storage
bucket is locked down.

00:01:02.770 --> 00:01:07.030
And it's only accessible to a
select few service accounts.

00:01:07.030 --> 00:01:11.660
For this example, we will
use this service account.

00:01:11.660 --> 00:01:12.570
Oh, no!

00:01:12.570 --> 00:01:15.470
An attacker gained access
to our service account

00:01:15.470 --> 00:01:18.008
and to the sensitive data.

00:01:18.008 --> 00:01:19.550
They've added the
compromised service

00:01:19.550 --> 00:01:22.160
account to an
unauthorized project

00:01:22.160 --> 00:01:25.640
and created a bucket to
exfiltrate data using the cloud

00:01:25.640 --> 00:01:27.980
storage API.

00:01:27.980 --> 00:01:29.550
Let's take a look.

00:01:29.550 --> 00:01:32.930
Here, I'm acting as
the malicious actor.

00:01:32.930 --> 00:01:34.490
I copy sensitive data--

00:01:34.490 --> 00:01:36.680
account numbers--
from the customer

00:01:36.680 --> 00:01:40.040
account data bucket to the
exfiltrated customer data

00:01:40.040 --> 00:01:42.500
bucket using the
compromised service account.

00:01:45.930 --> 00:01:48.490
The malicious actor copies
the data to their own bucket,

00:01:48.490 --> 00:01:51.640
and can now allow access
from outside the company.

00:01:51.640 --> 00:01:53.200
MAX SALTONSTALL:
That's bad, right?

00:01:53.200 --> 00:01:54.702
How do we protect against that?

00:01:54.702 --> 00:01:56.410
STEVE SOLEIMANI: We'll
create a perimeter

00:01:56.410 --> 00:01:58.450
with VPC Service Controls.

00:01:58.450 --> 00:02:01.900
This lets you lock down GCP
services, similar to how

00:02:01.900 --> 00:02:05.050
firewalls protect your servers.

00:02:05.050 --> 00:02:07.870
From the console, we'll
go to VPC Service Controls

00:02:07.870 --> 00:02:10.090
and select our organization.

00:02:10.090 --> 00:02:11.980
This perimeter
controls which projects

00:02:11.980 --> 00:02:15.760
can call on the GCP APIs,
specifically allowing

00:02:15.760 --> 00:02:18.160
only the ones you choose.

00:02:18.160 --> 00:02:19.700
I give it a name--

00:02:19.700 --> 00:02:23.080
in this case,
super-exfiltration protection--

00:02:23.080 --> 00:02:27.070
then choose the project
I want to protect.

00:02:27.070 --> 00:02:30.550
Finally, I'm going to choose
to restrict the storage API.

00:02:36.180 --> 00:02:38.490
Any storage API call
coming from outside

00:02:38.490 --> 00:02:40.730
that perimeter will fail.

00:02:40.730 --> 00:02:45.000
Now, only storage API calls
from inside this project

00:02:45.000 --> 00:02:47.085
will be allowed.

00:02:47.085 --> 00:02:47.910
Success!

00:02:47.910 --> 00:02:51.360
Our data is safe,
or at least safer.

00:02:51.360 --> 00:02:53.820
As you can see, you can
use VPC Service Controls

00:02:53.820 --> 00:02:58.560
to lock down your projects
and protect sensitive data.

00:02:58.560 --> 00:02:59.880
MAX SALTONSTALL: That's great!

00:02:59.880 --> 00:03:01.040
I'll go set that up today.

00:03:01.040 --> 00:03:02.498
And it keeps me
safe from attackers

00:03:02.498 --> 00:03:04.385
inside or outside my company.

00:03:04.385 --> 00:03:06.510
STEVE SOLEIMANI: This is
just one of the many tools

00:03:06.510 --> 00:03:08.160
that Google Cloud
provides to make

00:03:08.160 --> 00:03:11.835
sure your public cloud workloads
stay as private as you need.

00:03:11.835 --> 00:03:14.460
MAX SALTONSTALL: Subscribe below
